The Unification of Zong was the long process by which the politically fragmented kingdoms and sacred frontier polities of the [[Jade Peninsula]] were brought under the authority of a succession of Zhongyuan-based kings, forming the centralized [[Kingdom of Zong]]. In later imperial style, the united state was also described as the [[Jade Empire]], a name adopted when the final king proclaimed himself ruler of the entire peninsula after the last campaigns of conquest.

The surviving sources describe the unification as an evolving sequence of campaigns, dynastic marriages, and administrative mergers, spanning several centuries rather than a single war. Under the [[House of Jian]], Zhongyuan became the principal military power of the interior and launched the first invasion of northern [[Haizhou]]. Under the [[House of Yuanlong]], formed through the marriage union of the Zhongyuan line with [[Nanyang]], the royal court extended Zong authority across the coast, completed the frontier wars, subdued the sacred kingdom of [[Beilong]], and invaded [[Jiaozhi]] to recover the southern Haizhou districts that Jiaozhi had absorbed. These campaigns together produced the peninsula-wide consolidation that eventually became the [[Jade Empire]].

Later tradition also associated the end of the process with the creation of the [[Societal Assembly]], a court institution adopted from [[Nanyang]] and mandated by [[Tianwu of Zong|King Tianwu]] to prevent future fragmentation. The emperor remained the highest authority, but the assembly helped confirm the succession and gave the great families a controlled role in the political order.

== Background ==

The political world of the Jade Peninsula before unification was fragmented. In the older dynastic tradition preserved in the history of [[Kingdom of Donghai|Donghai]], the peninsula was divided among numerous regional kingdoms and maritime lordships that later coalesced into the zones known as [[Beilong]], [[Nanyang]], [[Haizhou]], and [[Jiaozhi]]. These polities competed with one another through trade, border warfare, and shifting alliances. Coastal regions remained important because of maritime commerce, while inland kingdoms controlled agricultural production and river systems. No single state possessed the authority to impose lasting unity over the peninsula.

The most unusual of the older polities was [[Beilong]], which had remained independent for nearly a thousand years. Beilong was respected by Zhongyuan rulers because it hosted the seat of the Tianist religion, and its sacred status gave it a legitimacy that other frontier kingdoms lacked. For generations, Zongyuan's kings treated Beilong as a protected and honored kingdom rather than a normal rival. This deference delayed conquest. As Beilong's ruling house entered a period of dynastic decline, the kingdom's sacred status proved insufficient protection against internal fragmentation and eventual Zong annexation.

By 894, the political balance in the south had already begun to break down. The court of [[Haizhou]] collapsed under financial exhaustion, and [[Jiaozhi]] invaded the weakened state, ending the long [[Great Peace]] that had bound [[Haizhou]], [[Nanyang]], and [[Jiaozhi]] into a regional buffer against Zhongyuan's repeated attempts to expand westward since the collapse of Donghai. The crisis destabilised the southern marches and made the later unification wars more likely. Debt, famine, and the collapse of cooperative defense arrangements increasingly left the frontier states unable to maintain their own armies.

At the same time, the northeastern frontier remained politically unstable. The later history of [[Mabatok]] describes a patchwork of island lordships, mixed port cities, and successor states that emerged after the collapse of Dagit rule. In that setting, the rising kingdom of [[Zong]] based in [[Zhongyuan]] came to represent the strongest concentration of military power on the peninsula.

== Dynasties and rulers ==

The unification era is traditionally divided into two dynastic phases. The [[House of Jian]] consolidated Zhongyuan and opened the first coastward campaigns. The [[House of Yuanlong]], formed through the marriage union with [[Nanyang]], extended Zong authority across the coast, subdued the remaining frontier kingdoms, and brought the process to its conclusion with the proclamation of the [[Jade Empire]].

== Detailed narrative ==

=== House of Jian ===

[[King Jianwen of Zhongyuan]] (r. c. 900–948) is traditionally credited with building the first permanent royal army and turning Zhongyuan into the principal military power of the inner peninsula. His court reorganised taxation, garrisoning, and river transport so that the kingdom could sustain repeated campaigns. Under his reign, the first wave of annexations began.

The first major external target was [[Haizhou]]. Jianwen launched the First Zong-Haizhou Campaign against the northern coast, taking the provinces of [[Linhai Province|Linhai]] and [[Beiyuan Province|Beiyuan]]. Linhai was the more maritime of the two provinces, while Beiyuan controlled inland routes and supply corridors. The campaign gave Zhongyuan a permanent foothold on the coast and broke Haizhou's northern shield without immediately destroying the state itself. Haizhou survived the campaign but was forced into a defensive posture from which it never fully recovered.

[[King Jianwu of Zhongyuan]] (r. c. 948–992) continued the project with a more explicit programme of conquest. He completed the subordination of the inland rivals and established the practice of annual campaign seasons, making unification a permanent feature of state policy rather than a temporary war effort. By the end of the House of Jian, Zhongyuan was the dominant military power on the peninsula, and the remaining regional states were compelled to respond to its expansion.

=== House of Yuanlong ===

The [[House of Yuanlong]] was formed through the marriage of [[King Longhai of Zong]] (r. c. 992–1066) to [[Princess Yulan of Nanyang]], daughter of [[King Yuanhe of Nanyang]]. The marriage created a personal union between the Zhongyuan and Nanyang courts, and the new house took its name from both lineages. Longhai shifted the centre of gravity of the kingdom toward the coast, strengthened the royal navy, and tied harbour cities more closely to the court at Zhongyuan. His reign extended the project of unification from the interior to the coast, making naval power a central element of Zong's consolidation strategy.

Nanyang retained its own law and aristocratic customs at first, but its princes, tax offices, and port officials were gradually drawn into the Zhongyuan administrative orbit. The union proceeded through marriage, inheritance, and the gradual absorption of Nanyang's administrative structures rather than outright military annexation.

[[King Longshan of Zong]] (r. c. 1066–1128) expanded the kingdom's reach further. He imposed royal control over key port settlements, appointed military governors to hold the coastline, and pressed the frontier kingdoms into submission. By the end of his reign, Zhongyuan had become the core of a peninsula-wide state.

[[King Tianrui of Zong]] (r. c. 1128–1210) intensified the frontier wars, extended royal administration into the far south, and prepared the campaigns that would bring the remaining maritime polities under Zong authority. His court increasingly described the peninsula as a single political body in need of complete consolidation.

The most significant obstacle facing the later House of Yuanlong was [[Beilong]]. Although Beilong had long been respected for its role as the seat of Tianist religion, its ruling house entered a period of dynastic decline. Court factions multiplied, succession disputes weakened local authority, and the kingdom's sacred prestige no longer provided effective protection against political instability. Tianrui and later Tianwu concluded that the Tianist seat could not remain outside imperial protection if the peninsula were to be permanently unified.

[[King Tianwu of Zong]] (r. c. 1210–1250) brought the long process to its conclusion. His reign combined religious legitimation, military pressure, and constitutional reform. The Beilong campaign was presented as the restoration of rightful custodianship over the Tianist seat, while the Zong-Jiaozhi War broke the last remaining centres of resistance in the frontier states. The southern campaign was led by [[Marshal Lanqiu]], whose armies pushed through the remaining coastal and inland strongholds while debt, famine, and alliance failure hollowed out the opposition.

Jiaozhi had previously absorbed the southern districts of Haizhou after Haizhou's financial collapse, making the Zong-Jiaozhi War both a reconquest of lost territory and a final act of peninsula-wide consolidation. Lanqiu's invasion moved through the old Haizhou borderlands first, then pressed deeper into Jiaozhi proper once the frontier defence had broken.

=== The final southern march invasion ===

The final wave of unification focused on the southern marches and coastal approaches. The campaign was not only military, but logistical: roads, harbors, and grain depots had to be secured so that Zong armies could move across the peninsula without losing their supply lines. The weakened states of the south had become increasingly dependent on debt financing and emergency taxation, which reduced their ability to sustain prolonged resistance.

Marshal Lanqiu's invasion broke the remaining coalition by moving rapidly along the southern corridor and preventing the frontier states from re-forming their defensive alliance. Once the coast and interior could no longer support one another, local elites began to negotiate surrender terms individually. The unification tradition accordingly describes the southern campaign as a march rather than a single battle, reflecting the sector-by-sector nature of the conquest rather than any single decisive engagement.

=== The reconquest of Mabatok ===

{{main|Mabatok}}

The final frontier challenge was [[Mabatok]], the northeastern archipelago and coastal strip that had long existed as a separate maritime region. In its fragmented late-medieval form, Mabatok was divided among island strongholds, mixed port cities, and interior successor states. The later Zong unification campaign treated this region as part of the broader effort to consolidate the Jade Peninsula under a single dynasty.

According to the Mabatok account, the Zong conquest of the region was the northeastern front of a wider campaign that also included Beilong, Haizhou, and Jiaozhi. The island fortresses proved difficult to take, but the peninsula-wide pressure created by Zhongyuan power eventually broke resistance. The fall of the last holdouts in Mabatok symbolised the end of regional fragmentation and the completion of Zong unification.

=== The Societal Assembly ===

In the final decades of the unification, the court adopted the Societal Assembly from Nanyang as a stabilising institution. The assembly had originally developed as an aristocratic senate in Nanyang, but Tianwu mandated its use at the imperial level as part of a broader settlement meant to prevent another breakup. Noble houses, senior officials, and selected commercial interests were drawn into the chamber, which confirmed the emperor while still preserving the emperor's ultimate authority.

The assembly did not replace royal power. Instead, it formalised the court-state that emerged from unification. The king remained supreme, but succession now depended on institutional confirmation rather than simple dynastic assertion.

== Proclamation of the Jade Empire ==

At the end of the conquest, the final Zhongyuan king assumed imperial style and proclaimed himself ruler of the entire Jade Peninsula. In later tradition, this moment marked the transition from the Kingdom of Zong to the [[Jade Empire]]. The new title reflected not only military victory but also a claim to universal authority over the peninsula's peoples, trade routes, and frontier zones.

The proclamation did not erase regional diversity, but it established a new political order. Former rival kingdoms were subordinated to a centralized court, and Zhongyuan became the core from which law, military power, and administrative authority radiated outward. The unification therefore marked both the end of the peninsula's long fragmentation and the beginning of a more durable imperial state.

== Legacy ==

The Unification of Zong had lasting consequences for the political development of eastern Fosia. It established the basic structure of the Zongic state: a strong central monarchy, a unified administrative tradition, and a strategic orientation toward both inland consolidation and maritime power. The era also created the historical memory of Zhongyuan as the origin point of Zong statehood.

In Zongic historiography, the unification is treated as the foundational period of the Kingdom of Zong. The united kingdom became the dominant political and historical framework for the peninsula, persisting as a reference point even in regions that retained distinct local identities. The title of [[Jade Empire]] preserved that memory of conquest and centralization, linking the Zhongyuan kings' campaigns to the larger imperial identity that followed.

